WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:02.968
-Welcome back to the show.
-Thank you.

00:00:02.968 --> 00:00:04.767
-I always love to see --
Yeah, we love you.

00:00:04.767 --> 00:00:07.234
I always love seeing you, pal.
I want to say congratulations.

00:00:07.234 --> 00:00:09.234
You co-wrote and directed
your first movie.

00:00:09.234 --> 00:00:10.801
-I did.
-This is a big deal.

00:00:10.801 --> 00:00:12.734
-Thank you.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:12.734 --> 00:00:14.200
Yeah.
-Alright.

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:16.801
What is it called and what
can you tell us about it?

00:00:16.801 --> 00:00:18.167
-It's called "Hell of a Summer."

00:00:18.167 --> 00:00:23.300
It's like a slasher horror
but -- with a lot of blood.

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:27.701
But it also has -- It's supposed
to be funny, so I hope it is.

00:00:27.701 --> 00:00:30.067
-Yeah. I think you can do both.
-Yeah.

00:00:30.067 --> 00:00:32.133
-Were you nervous
about directing or...?

00:00:32.133 --> 00:00:36.067
-Definitely, but I also --
I've always wanted to be, like,

00:00:36.067 --> 00:00:38.133
making films
since I was really little.

00:00:38.133 --> 00:00:40.567
And now
I'm a little less little.

00:00:40.567 --> 00:00:42.868
-Yeah.
-So, it makes -- Yeah.

00:00:42.868 --> 00:00:44.734
I feel like it makes sense.
-Did you talk to

00:00:44.734 --> 00:00:47.000
the Duffer brothers and say,
"Hey, give me some advice.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:49.067
What do I do?"
These guys are masters.

00:00:49.067 --> 00:00:51.100
-Yeah, totally.
-Writing and directing.

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:53.400
-Yeah, I texted them,
and I was like, "Do you guys

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:55.567
ever get imposter syndrome still
when you're directing?"

00:00:55.567 --> 00:01:00.234
And they were like, "Yeah."
So they still get...

00:01:00.234 --> 00:01:01.667
-So, if they get it --
Then, yeah.

00:01:01.667 --> 00:01:03.567
-Then we're good.
-But you're also acting in it.

00:01:03.567 --> 00:01:04.634
-Yeah.

00:01:04.634 --> 00:01:06.234
-So you're better
than the Duffer brothers.

00:01:06.234 --> 00:01:08.901
-No, no, no, no, no.
Well, actual--

00:01:08.901 --> 00:01:10.801
You know, maybe --

00:01:10.801 --> 00:01:12.234
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.

00:01:12.234 --> 00:01:13.634
-'Cause they don't act.

00:01:13.634 --> 00:01:15.434
-You know what?
-Yeah.

00:01:15.434 --> 00:01:17.634
-The Duffer brothers have never
acted in their own stuff.

00:01:17.634 --> 00:01:19.200
And that's why
I'm better than them.

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:20.501
-Yes! Exactly right!

00:01:20.501 --> 00:01:22.934
Thank you! Finally!
You can say it on this show!

00:01:22.934 --> 00:01:25.767
-That's not actually true
because when I was acting in it,

00:01:25.767 --> 00:01:27.868
I was like,
"Man, this is going so well."

00:01:27.868 --> 00:01:29.701
Then I got into the edit room,
and I realized --

00:01:29.701 --> 00:01:31.634
I was, like, watching it,
and I was like,

00:01:31.634 --> 00:01:33.934
"Whoa.
I'm not acting in that shot.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:35.501
I'm not acting in that shot."

00:01:35.501 --> 00:01:38.701
It's just basically me
watching the movie like this.

00:01:38.701 --> 00:01:40.434
-You're reading the lines?
-Yeah.

00:01:40.434 --> 00:01:41.667
I'm, like, mouthing people --

00:01:41.667 --> 00:01:43.367
I'm mouthing along
with people's lines.

00:01:43.367 --> 00:01:45.033
-You couldn't get out
of your director...

00:01:45.033 --> 00:01:47.501
-No, apparently not.
-Oh, my gosh.

00:01:47.501 --> 00:01:48.968
-So, yeah, I don't know
if I could maybe do that again.

00:01:48.968 --> 00:01:50.701
-Dude, I'm excited.
I can't wait.

00:01:50.701 --> 00:01:52.667
When that comes out,
please come on the show.

00:01:52.667 --> 00:01:53.868
We'll talk about it.
-Thank you. I'd love to.

00:01:53.868 --> 00:01:55.667
-I haven't seen you
since season four

00:01:55.667 --> 00:01:57.601
of "Stranger Things" came out.
Congratulations.

00:01:57.601 --> 00:01:59.534
It's the biggest thing
in the world.

00:01:59.534 --> 00:02:00.834
-Thank you.

00:02:00.834 --> 00:02:02.501
-So well done.
-Thanks.

00:02:02.501 --> 00:02:07.234
-And kudos to the whole cast
and the Duffers, as well.

00:02:07.234 --> 00:02:11.367
And season five -- this is
the last season coming up.

00:02:11.367 --> 00:02:12.968
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:02:12.968 --> 00:02:14.834
Are you nervous, excited, sad,

00:02:14.834 --> 00:02:16.567
or just waiting
for all those emotions?

00:02:16.567 --> 00:02:18.434
-It's going to be crazy
once it's all done.

00:02:18.434 --> 00:02:20.968
I'm just excited to go back
and actually finish it.

00:02:20.968 --> 00:02:24.667
-They released the name
of the first episode.

00:02:24.667 --> 00:02:26.601
-Yeah.
-It's called "The Crawl."

00:02:26.601 --> 00:02:29.300
-I'm not even joking. I found
out about that yesterday.

00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:30.467
-Really?
-Yeah.

00:02:30.467 --> 00:02:32.300
-So you don't know?
They just did it,

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:34.767
and you go, "Oh, when are you
guys going to tell me?"

00:02:34.767 --> 00:02:36.634
-No, and I think a lot of people
don't believe it

00:02:36.634 --> 00:02:38.200
because it just is, like,
they don't --

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:39.801
"Of course you know.
You're on the show."

00:02:39.801 --> 00:02:41.734
But, like, they're really --
they're super cagey.

00:02:41.734 --> 00:02:45.467
-Yeah. For a good reason.
-I guess so, yeah.

00:02:45.467 --> 00:02:49.067
Or else I'd go blabbing
on "The Tonight Show."

00:02:49.067 --> 00:02:50.667
-Yeah. Exactly.

00:02:50.667 --> 00:02:53.634
But I heard also that you --
You have a creative brain.

00:02:53.634 --> 00:02:56.334
You have a very artistic brain,
a great brain.

00:02:56.334 --> 00:03:00.934
And I think you said, as a joke
to someone on the staff

00:03:00.934 --> 00:03:02.934
or on the crew -- You were
doing "Stranger Things."

00:03:02.934 --> 00:03:05.200
You go, "You should do a
spin-off of blah, blah, blah."

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:07.133
And you said something,
and they were like,

00:03:07.133 --> 00:03:09.033
"You're kind of right."
-Well, yeah.

00:03:09.033 --> 00:03:12.934
So, we were on set filming
"Stranger Things 4."

00:03:12.934 --> 00:03:14.667
And we were all
talking about, like, yeah,

00:03:14.667 --> 00:03:16.701
if there's going to be, like --
Like, joking.

00:03:16.701 --> 00:03:18.367
"Oh, they're all going to
have us back in 20 years.

00:03:18.367 --> 00:03:19.734
We're all going to be,
like, fat and old

00:03:19.734 --> 00:03:21.167
and being like,
'Oh, Evan, where are you?'"

00:03:21.167 --> 00:03:23.100
You know, that kind of thing.

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:26.501
And then I was like,
"Oh, but, if you guys are

00:03:26.501 --> 00:03:28.267
actually going to do a spin-off,
it should be this."

00:03:28.267 --> 00:03:29.701
And I said it,
and the Duffers, like,

00:03:29.701 --> 00:03:31.567
looked at each other and looked
at me, and they were like,

00:03:31.567 --> 00:03:33.100
"Could we talk to you
for a second?"

00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:34.968
And then they pulled me off
and they were like,

00:03:34.968 --> 00:03:36.767
"That is the idea.
Who told you?"

00:03:36.767 --> 00:03:38.767
I was like, "No one."

00:03:38.767 --> 00:03:40.000
They were like,
"What do you mean?

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:41.234
You just, like,
came up with it?"

00:03:41.234 --> 00:03:42.968
And I was like, "Well, no,
I just thought that that

00:03:42.968 --> 00:03:45.300
would be a cool way
to kind of expand..."

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:46.601
And they were like...
-It is.

00:03:46.601 --> 00:03:48.701
-Yeah. They were like,
"Well, yeah, I don't know."

00:03:48.701 --> 00:03:50.400
And they were like --
Yeah, it was really funny.

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:53.067
They were like,
"Okay, well, don't tell anyone."

00:03:53.067 --> 00:03:54.767
[ Laughter ]

00:03:54.767 --> 00:03:57.367
-"And it's our idea."
-Yeah, exactly.

00:03:57.367 --> 00:03:59.501
-I want to talk
about your new movie.

00:03:59.501 --> 00:04:01.667
It's called "When You Finish
Saving the World."

00:04:01.667 --> 00:04:03.734
It was written and directed
by Jesse Eisenberg.

00:04:03.734 --> 00:04:06.501
Gosh, we love that guy.
-Yeah, he's one of the best.

00:04:06.501 --> 00:04:07.934
-Oh, my gosh. He's brilliant.

00:04:07.934 --> 00:04:10.501
How did you get involved
with this one?

00:04:10.501 --> 00:04:13.767
-Jesse had written
an Audible Original

00:04:13.767 --> 00:04:15.267
sort of, like, a --

00:04:15.267 --> 00:04:19.601
kind of audiobook play
sort of thing and put me in it.

00:04:19.601 --> 00:04:22.367
And it was based on that.
The movie is based on that.

00:04:22.367 --> 00:04:23.501
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:04:23.501 --> 00:04:25.100
It's with Julianne Moore.

00:04:25.100 --> 00:04:26.767
It's about this kind of
mother and son

00:04:26.767 --> 00:04:29.701
who are complete opposites.

00:04:29.701 --> 00:04:32.200
Yeah. I don't know.
It's an honor to be a part of.

00:04:32.200 --> 00:04:35.067
-You also play, like,
an aspiring musician in this.

00:04:35.067 --> 00:04:36.434
-Yes.

00:04:36.434 --> 00:04:38.300
-And, really in real life,
you are a musician.

00:04:38.300 --> 00:04:39.567
You're a great musician,
by the way.

00:04:39.567 --> 00:04:41.033
I was telling you --
I was listening to you

00:04:41.033 --> 00:04:42.968
on Spotify earlier today.
-Yeah. Thank you.

00:04:42.968 --> 00:04:44.734
-You wrote some of the songs
for this?

00:04:44.734 --> 00:04:46.567
-Yeah, I helped write
some of the songs.

00:04:46.567 --> 00:04:49.167
There's like two or three songs
in the movie that I wrote,

00:04:49.167 --> 00:04:51.467
but they're less important.

00:04:51.467 --> 00:04:54.567
They're the sort of shallow,
bad ones, and then --

00:04:54.567 --> 00:04:56.100
-No, it's great. They're great.

00:04:56.100 --> 00:04:57.601
-No, but then the great ones --

00:04:57.601 --> 00:05:00.300
Like, Emile Mosseri,
who's one of the greatest

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:02.200
living film composers,

00:05:02.200 --> 00:05:04.534
helped, you know --
wrote it with Jesse.

00:05:04.534 --> 00:05:07.100
And Jesse did the lyrics
and everything.

00:05:07.100 --> 00:05:09.901
But, yeah, no, it was amazing
to sort of do both.

00:05:09.901 --> 00:05:13.667
Like, be kind of treated
as a collaborator.

00:05:13.667 --> 00:05:15.367
-Does that change your --

00:05:15.367 --> 00:05:18.067
the way you write your songs,
meeting these composers?

00:05:18.067 --> 00:05:20.667
-Yeah, definitely. I remember
just being like, "Oh, man.

00:05:20.667 --> 00:05:22.868
How similar
are my songs to this,

00:05:22.868 --> 00:05:25.400
like, really arrogant
kid's songs?"

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:28.067
-Yeah.
-Yeah, so I started comparing --

00:05:28.067 --> 00:05:30.234
The guy that I play,
his name is, like, Ziggy.

00:05:30.234 --> 00:05:33.300
And he's, like,
this pretty sweet kid

00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:35.567
but also super-obnoxious
and arrogant.

00:05:35.567 --> 00:05:38.133
And, you know,
he writes these songs

00:05:38.133 --> 00:05:40.667
and plays them live
on the Internet

00:05:40.667 --> 00:05:42.501
kind of like
on a TikTok platform.

00:05:42.501 --> 00:05:44.400
But, like, no one cares
in real life about him

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:45.968
or about what he's doing.

00:05:45.968 --> 00:05:47.634
So he's always just like,
"Yeah, so, I have

00:05:47.634 --> 00:05:49.667
like 20,000 followers,
so it's pretty cool."

00:05:49.667 --> 00:05:52.667
And everyone's like,
"We don't care, dude."

00:05:52.667 --> 00:05:54.067
-Yeah.

00:05:54.067 --> 00:05:55.968
-But it was very --
Yeah, it's great.

00:05:55.968 --> 00:05:57.634
-What is it?
It's under Ziggy Katz?

00:05:57.634 --> 00:05:59.000
-Yeah, Ziggy Katz.

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:00.667
-Ziggy Katz is what
I was listening to today.

00:06:00.667 --> 00:06:02.267
-K-A-T-Z.
-And the song?

00:06:02.267 --> 00:06:03.801
-It's called "Pieces of Gold."

00:06:03.801 --> 00:06:05.334
-Just check it out.
It's so good.

00:06:05.334 --> 00:06:07.868
Dude, I love it, top to bottom.
I want to show everyone a clip.

00:06:07.868 --> 00:06:10.434
Here's Finn Wolfhard in "When
You Finish Saving the World."

00:06:10.434 --> 00:06:11.968
Take a look at this.

00:06:11.968 --> 00:06:15.133
-♪ That's the way
the earthquake crumbles ♪

00:06:15.133 --> 00:06:17.033
-Hello?!

00:06:17.033 --> 00:06:18.367
[ Drill whirs ]

00:06:18.367 --> 00:06:19.801
What the hell are you doing?

00:06:19.801 --> 00:06:21.167
-Installing a red beacon light

00:06:21.167 --> 00:06:23.033
so people know
when I'm live-streaming.

00:06:23.033 --> 00:06:24.834
-And by "people," you mean...
-I mean you.

00:06:24.834 --> 00:06:27.501
-Well, I figured that, yes.
-And to a lesser extent, Dad.

00:06:27.501 --> 00:06:29.667
-Well, it's nice to be first.

00:06:29.667 --> 00:06:32.000
-Yeah, so,
when this light is on,

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:34.801
you don't knock or come in
or make any noise.

00:06:34.801 --> 00:06:36.334
-Oh, and when it's off, I am --

00:06:36.334 --> 00:06:38.400
I'm free to go
about my business,

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:40.734
to enjoy the rich
pageantry of life?

00:06:40.734 --> 00:06:43.234
-What? Yeah, sure.

00:06:43.234 --> 00:06:46.067
-It's a good system.

00:06:46.067 --> 00:06:48.968
-Yeah! Julianne Moore.

00:06:48.968 --> 00:06:51.567
We love her.
Finn Wolfhard, everybody.

00:06:51.567 --> 00:06:53.267
"When You Finish
Saving the World"

00:06:53.267 --> 00:06:55.267
is in theaters everywhere
January 20th.

00:06:55.267 --> 00:06:57.234
Meghann Fahy joins us
after the break.

00:06:57.234 --> 00:06:58.801
Stick around, everybody.

